From b2a5ed8b92ed103f62baa46651e82651e2d48982 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: royka1 <397414+royka1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:58:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Qualcomm iris support, Firefox and Chromium zero-copy

Decoding (all bit-exact against software decoding on the Diting iris
decoder: H.264 with B-frames, HEVC, VP9 incl. alt-ref and YouTube VP9):
- Record the real CAPTURE layout (one-plane NV12, aligned stride/rows)
  and use it for GetImage, DeriveImage and surface export.
- Map pictures to surfaces by V4L2 timestamp instead of dequeue order;
  hidden VP9 frames (show_frame = 0) produce no picture.
- Request decode-order output (display delay controls), so every
  picture arrives without waiting for later input.
- H.264: take the PPS default reference counts from the first P/B slice
  and resend the PPS when it changes.
- Reserve buffer IDs before use (race), free image buffers on destroy.
- Collect pictures while waiting for a free OUTPUT buffer (deadlock).
- Detach surfaces when their context is destroyed (use after free).

VA-API conformance:
- QueryConfigAttributes reports the RT format; QuerySurfaceAttributes
  reports min/max width and height and no longer overflows the
  caller's array.
- ExportSurfaceHandle fills DRM_PRIME_2 descriptors with real offsets
  and pitches; the caller owns the fd.

Chromium (exports surfaces before decoding, never calls vaSyncSurface):
- Such surfaces get their own GBM buffer in the decoder layout, and the
  CAPTURE queue switches to DMABUF so the decoder writes into them
  directly. One buffer is queued at a time, for the oldest waiting
  frame: the firmware returns empty buffers for hidden frames early,
  which would shift a deeper queue.
- vaEndPicture waits for the picture of such surfaces, since Chromium
  displays them right away.
- V4L2VA_OWN_BUFFERS=1 forces this path for testing with ffmpeg.

Firefox: ExportSurfaceHandle waits for the picture (Firefox exports
before vaSyncSurface).

